    "# Summary and conculsions"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "A cambered NACA 4-digit airfoil was generated using randomly selected values of camber, camber location, and thickness. The upper and lower surfaces were then reconstructed using a user-defined Lagrange interpolation function with a limited number of data points.\n",
    "\n",
    "The reconstruction accuracy was evaluated for $5$, $10$, and $15$ interpolation points using the maximum error, RMS error, and RMS percentage error. Among these cases, $10$ points gave the lowest RMS error. However, increasing the number of points to $15$ did not further improve the results, showing that a higher number of points does not always guarantee better accuracy.\n",
    "\n",
    "The study also considered the effect of data-point spacing on the reconstruction. Uniform spacing distributes the points at equal intervals along the chord, whereas cosine spacing places more points near the leading and trailing edges, where the airfoil curvature changes more rapidly. This makes cosine spacing a useful alternative for capturing the airfoil shape near these regions. A comparison of the two methods can help determine which spacing provides better accuracy for a given number of points.\n",
    "\n",
    "Overall, the results highlight that the accuracy of Lagrange interpolation depends on both the number and distribution of data points. Choosing a suitable point spacing, along with an appropriate number of interpolation points, can improve the reconstruction of the airfoil surface."
   ]
  },
